Datool licensing
================

Datool-authored content is licensed under the Apache License 2.0, except for
Datool Build content expressly identified below. The full Apache license is
in LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt. Third-party components retain their original
licenses and notices.

Datool Build
------------

The following directories and all their contents are licensed under the
Elastic License 2.0 (ELv2), whose complete, unmodified text is in
LICENSES/Elastic-2.0.txt. Directory names below are literal, including square
brackets in Next.js route names:

    app/(app)/p/[projectSlug]/build/
    app/api/build/
    app/api/build-public/
    app/api/build-webhooks/
    app/chat/
    components/build/
    src/lib/build/
    src/server/build/

The following individual files are also licensed under ELv2:

    src/server/metrics/build.ts
    src/browser/widget-runtime.tsx
    src/browser/widget-runtime.css
    scripts/build-worker.ts
    scripts/test-build-e2e.ts
    scripts/test-whatsapp-e2e.ts
    public/datool-widget.js
    public/widget-runtime.js
    public/widget-runtime.css
    tests/helpers/build-documents.ts
    docs/build.md
    content/docs/guides/whatsapp.mdx

The following file patterns identify additional Build-specific files licensed
under ELv2. Here, * matches zero or more characters within one path segment:

    migrations/*_build_*.sql
    migrations/*_whatsapp_*.sql
    scripts/build-e2e-*.ts
    tests/build-*.test.ts
    tests/build-*.test.tsx

These notices cover Datool's portions of generated Build assets; bundled
third-party code retains its own licenses. Moving or copying Build code does
not remove its ELv2 terms or notices.

Apache 2.0 components
---------------------

Observe, Test, the TypeScript and Python SDKs, the CLI, and all other
Datool-authored files outside the Build scope above remain Apache 2.0. This
includes shared UI, authentication, project management, and observability
infrastructure. Build-specific simulations inside src/server/build/
  src/server/metrics/build.ts are part
of Build, not the Apache-licensed Test product.

Separate licenses apply to separate portions. Build is not dual-licensed
under Apache 2.0 and ELv2. Accessing Build through an Apache-licensed client or
shared interface does not grant an Apache license to Build's implementation.

Commercial option and existing rights
-------------------------------------

A separate commercial license for hosted or managed Build offerings that
ELv2 does not permit is available by written agreement with the applicable
rights holder. See LICENSING.md for usage examples and how to inquire.
This notice does not itself grant commercial exceptions to ELv2.

This split does not revoke rights already granted for versions previously
distributed under Apache 2.0. Third-party rights and notices are preserved.
